=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The Linux netfilter FORWARD chain on **router6**, applied to its WAN-facing interface **eth2** (IP 10.50.1.2, the r6_r3 backbone link connecting to router3 at 10.50.1.1).

**Mechanism:** Three DROP rules are installed in router6's FORWARD chain that silently discard all TCP (proto 6), all UDP (proto 17), and all ICMP (proto 0) arriving on eth2 — regardless of source or destination. The only exception is a narrow permit for TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1. Because every packet from the 172.16.x.x side (Group 1: laptop0, laptop2, laptop3 on switch1) must traverse the backbone and enter router6 via eth2 to reach the 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 networks (Group 2: laptop4, laptop5, and attached workstations/printers on mswitch2/switch11/switch10), all such packets are silently dropped. Return traffic (from Group 2 toward Group 1) is similarly killed when the reply packets re-enter eth2. The ICMP DROP rule (rule 4) already shows 12 packet hits — exactly the pings sent during diagnosis — confirming active dropping. Intra-group pings (laptop4↔laptop5) succeed normally because they never traverse eth2.

**Root cause:** Three overly broad iptables DROP rules were added to router6's FORWARD chain on interface eth2, blocking all inter-group IP traffic in both directions between the 172.16.x.x and 172.18.x.x segments.

**Corrective fix:** On router6, remove the three DROP rules (highest number first to avoid renumbering): `iptables -D FORWARD 4; iptables -D FORWARD 3; iptables -D FORWARD 2`. To make the fix persistent across reboots, remove the same rules from `/etc/iptables/rules.v4` or the equivalent startup script.
=== END ===